WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
How do you study a gene a cell cannot live without?

2
00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.840
Break it permanently, and cells stop growing before you can test them.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.860
You need a switch you can flip on demand.

4
00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:17.830
A temperature-sensitive mutant builds that switch into one protein.

5
00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.770
At a low temperature, it works, so budding yeast divide normally.

6
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:28.990
Each bud shows the stage: none in G1, small in S, large by mitosis.

7
00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.010
Warm cells to the restrictive temperature, and that protein fails.

8
00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.810
Cells then stall with one uniform bud shape.

9
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:41.283
Never assume a gene acts where cells stop.

10
00:00:42.743 --> 00:00:45.043
Picture a car filling at one station each lap.

11
00:00:46.203 --> 00:00:49.763
Close that station beforehand, and it stalls later that lap.

12
00:00:50.943 --> 00:00:54.843
The station is the execution point, when the gene is last needed.

13
00:00:56.143 --> 00:00:59.383
Where cells collect is the termination point, or arrest point.

14
00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:03.317
Picture a growing culture spread around the cycle.

15
00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:08.797
We raise the heat right after one cell passes the execution point.

16
00:01:09.977 --> 00:01:13.817
Does it stop on this lap, or finish this division and stall next lap?

17
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:22.687
It finishes this lap, because it already passed the station.

18
00:01:23.887 --> 00:01:25.727
Only on the next lap does it stall.

19
00:01:27.007 --> 00:01:32.167
Follow single cells after the shift: those past the station finish, those before it

20
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:36.987
stall. So the fraction that finish locates the execution point.

21
00:01:38.207 --> 00:01:42.887
Switch an essential protein off with heat, and how cells stall reveals when it acts.

22
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:45.293
Two traps can mislead.

23
00:01:46.613 --> 00:01:50.573
First, stalled cells show the arrest point, not when the gene acted.

24
00:01:52.113 --> 00:01:54.573
Track single cells or pulse heat to find timing.

25
00:01:55.873 --> 00:02:00.133
Second, a screen's count of genes is its yield, not every gene needed.

26
00:02:01.313 --> 00:02:06.453
A double mutant looks like whichever single mutant blocks earlier, so double mutants

27
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:07.453
order steps.

28
00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:11.687
With those guards, heat switches unlocked major pathways.

29
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:18.607
In 1970, Leland Hartwell, Joseph Culotti and Brian Reid described

30
00:02:18.607 --> 00:02:23.027
temperature-sensitive mutants of budding yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae.

31
00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:27.127
They called them cdc, for cell division cycle.

32
00:02:28.427 --> 00:02:31.787
At a higher temperature, each stopped with one uniform shape.

33
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:40.540
In 1979, Novick and Schekman reported sec1, a yeast mutant growing at 25 °C.

34
00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:47.540
At 37 °C, it keeps making secretory enzymes and cannot release them.

35
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:56.100
In 1980, Novick, Field and Schekman sorted 188 clones trapping the enzyme invertase

36
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.260
into 23 complementation groups.

37
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:00.940
Each is roughly one gene.

38
00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:05.060
Double mutants ordered those steps in 1981.

39
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.440
Heat switches work in animals too.

40
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:15.960
In 1990, Volker Hartenstein and James Posakony warmed fly pupae carrying a

41
00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.660
temperature-sensitive version of Notch, a signaling protein.

42
00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:27.040
Pulses between 0 and 14 hours after pupae formed removed Notch for chosen windows.

43
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.640
Extra bristle precursors formed in place of skin cells.

44
00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:35.280
So heat timing reveals when a protein acts.
